string getStoreMaterialRequirements(string skipCountTxt)
{
	// Everything the store person is owed, grouped by supervisor, with fabric
	// requirements already allocated against reusable offcuts.
	//
	// CONTRACT CHANGED: was a bare JSON array, now
	// {"plans":[...supervisor blocks, same shape as before...],"plansConsumed":N}.
	// A PLAN-COUNT page size (PAGE_SIZE=20, then 10) was tried first and
	// proved wrong, not just imperfect: statement cost tracks
	// Material_Requirement ROW count, and row count per plan is arbitrary
	// (however many items x however many BOM lines), so a fixed number of
	// PLANS is not a fixed amount of WORK. Two pages of 10 plans each can
	// differ by 10x in actual cost depending on which plans land on which
	// page - proven here by Execute: page 1 succeeded at pageSize=20, page 2
	// (same size) failed, twice, even after fixing two separate unbounded
	// fetches (Waste_Master, Fabric_Piece) that turned out to be the bigger
	// cost. Shrinking pageSize further would only ever be a coin flip that
	// happens to work for THIS dataset and fails again the moment order mix
	// changes - not a fix.
	//
	// So the boundary moved to what actually drives cost:
	// Material_Requirement ROW COUNT, walked and capped by ROW_BUDGET below.
	// Plans are consumed ONE AT A TIME (in small fetch batches, so the
	// SCANNING itself stays bounded) until the running row total would
	// exceed the budget, and the call stops there - however many or few
	// plans that turned out to be. plansConsumed tells the caller exactly
	// how many top-priority plans this call walked, so the NEXT call's
	// skipCountTxt is skipCountTxt + plansConsumed - not a page number,
	// because pages are no longer a fixed size and a page number cannot
	// describe where a variable-size walk stopped.
	//
	// ROW_BUDGET = 150. Not derived from a formula - chosen with real margin
	// under the volumes that were tested to fail (a 20-plan page could
	// exceed several hundred rows once waste/lot/material passes are
	// included; 150 keeps this call's own aggregation loop, plus everything
	// downstream that scales with neededMats/lotMats, comfortably inside the
	// statement ceiling proven safe by the pageSize=10 test). If a single
	// PLAN alone ever carries more than ROW_BUDGET requirement rows, that one
	// plan is still consumed whole (never split mid-plan - a partial
	// aggregation would double-count on the next call) and plansConsumed=1
	// for that call; whether ROW_BUDGET holds against a plan that size has
	// not been tested and would need widening if it doesn't.
	//
	// skipCountTxt arrives as a STRING from the Custom API, and empty/invalid
	// must default to 0 (start from the top), not throw. The signature and
	// contract both changed here, so the Custom API config in Creator needs
	// its argument name AND its callers updated to match, per CLAUDE.md.
	rowBudget = 150;
	planFetchBatch = 5;
	skipCount = 0;
	scStr = ifnull(skipCountTxt,"").toString().trim();
	if(scStr != "")
	{
		skipCount = scStr.toLong();
		if(skipCount < 0)
		{
			skipCount = 0;
		}
	}
	plansConsumed = 0;
	rowCountSoFar = 0;
	resultJson = "[]";

	try
	{

	agg = Map();
	orderedKeys = List();

	// Which materials this screen actually asks about. Collected as the
	// requirements are aggregated - it costs one contains() per row - and used to
	// throw away irrelevant lots before any work is done on them. A store with
	// 270 lots but 15 fabrics on screen would otherwise pay full price for 255 of
	// them on every single fetch.
	neededMats = List();

	// Plan_Item name and remake flag, cached by id. Lines are per requirement
	// ROW, so one order can contribute two of them - and since a QC remake
	// repeats its original's material and order number, the two were arriving
	// as identical-looking rows with nothing to tell them apart.
	//
	// Cached rather than queried per row: a plan has a handful of items and the
	// same one appears on every requirement it owns, so this is a few queries
	// per call instead of one per line.
	piCache = Map();

	// HIGHEST PRIORITY FIRST, oldest first within a priority. This ordering does
	// TWO jobs and they are easy to mistake for one:
	//   - it is the order the supervisor cards render in, so the shopify
	//     supervisor is at the top of the store person's screen;
	//   - it is the order the widget claims offcuts and lots in, so when two
	//     supervisors want the same remnant the shopify one gets it.
	// Both fall out of this single sort: supOrder is built as the keys are
	// walked, and applyLotAllocation spends the rack down card by card.
	//
	// Priority_Key = rank * 1000000 + plan sequence, stamped by
	// createProductionPlans. It is one field because Deluge `sort by` accepts
	// exactly one - there is no `sort by Priority_Rank, Added_Time` - and this
	// needed rank first with age as the tie-break. Added_Time is no longer read
	// here at all; the sequence half of the key carries the age.
	//
	// A plan created before Priority_Key existed has an EMPTY key and would sort
	// to one end or the other. They must be backfilled, not left to chance.
	//
	// "sort by" has to sit on its own assignment - Deluge will not parse it
	// inline in a for-each header.
	// Partially Received counts as still open. A plan whose first item has been
	// verified drops out of "Pending", and filtering on that alone made the rest
	// of its materials unissuable - invisible here and refused by issueMaterials.
	//
	// In Progress for the same reason: one item can be on the cutting table while
	// another is still waiting for cloth. Fully-issued rows render as read-only
	// receipts, so a plan appearing here does not mean it is asking for anything.
	// SLICED TO ONE PAGE AT THE QUERY ITSELF, same sorted order as before - the
	// priority ordering (highest rank, oldest first) is unchanged, only how
	// much of it any single call has to process.
	//
	// "range from X to Y" MUST sit on the SAME query expression as the fetch,
	// exactly like allPlansDesc does in createProductionPlans - splitting it
	// into "fetch everything, then range over the in-memory result" as an
	// earlier draft of this change did defeats the entire point: the
	// expensive part (119 rows) would already have happened before range even
	// runs.
	//
	// "range from X to Y" WITH VARIABLES - CONFIRMED WORKING against this org
	// by a throwaway test function (returned exactly the requested slice
	// size).
	//
	// ONE FETCH, up to SCAN_CAP=200 plans past skipCount - not the whole
	// remaining queue, and not the earlier batch-of-5-at-a-time design
	// (which needed a literal numeric-range list to drive the outer loop;
	// Deluge's List() takes no inline arguments, so that failed at Execute -
	// "Not able to find 'List' function" - and rather than guess at a second
	// unverified range/counter syntax, this collapses to the single query
	// shape already proven safe elsewhere in this file). SCAN_CAP bounds the
	// FETCH; rowBudget below (via the "if budgetSpent" guard) bounds how
	// much of what was fetched is actually WALKED into aggregation - so a
	// queue deeper than 200 is still safe, just scanned in more than one
	// call, exactly like scanLimit does in createProductionPlans.
	scanCap = 200;
	planBatch = Production_Planning[Order_Status == "Pending" || Order_Status == "Partially Received" || Order_Status == "In Progress"] sort by Priority_Key range from (skipCount + 1) to (skipCount + scanCap);

	budgetSpent = false;

	for each  plan in planBatch
	{
		if(budgetSpent == false)
		{
			planRows = Material_Requirement[Plan == plan.ID];
			thisPlanRowCount = planRows.count();

			// A plan is NEVER split mid-aggregation - partially aggregating
			// one plan's rows and picking up the rest on the next call would
			// double-count that plan the moment the next call re-fetches it.
			// So the budget check happens BEFORE this plan's rows are
			// touched, and if it already has 0 rows counted so far, this
			// plan is consumed whole regardless of its own size (see the
			// ROW_BUDGET note above the top of this function for what that
			// means if one plan alone exceeds the budget).
			if(rowCountSoFar > 0 && (rowCountSoFar + thisPlanRowCount) > rowBudget)
			{
				budgetSpent = true;
			}
			else
			{
				rowCountSoFar = rowCountSoFar + thisPlanRowCount;
				plansConsumed = plansConsumed + 1;

				// UNVERIFIED AGAINST THIS ORG - NEEDS A REAL EXECUTE, NOT
				// JUST A READ. Sales_Order is a Lookup field on
				// Production_Planning, and Deluge lookup dot-notation is
				// documented to read a linked record's field directly
				// (Lookup.Field) without a separate query - see
				// https://www.zoho.com/deluge/help/composite-fields-usage.html.
				// If Execute shows plan.Sales_Order.Sales_Order does not
				// resolve (blank, or throws), revert to a for-each
				// Sales_Order[ID == plan.Sales_Order] form - do not guess
				// further in code, test it directly.
				soNo = ifnull(plan.Sales_Order.Sales_Order,"").toString();

				for each  mr in planRows
				{
			supId = mr.Assigned_To;
			matId = mr.Material;
			if(supId != null && matId != null)
			{
				isFab = mr.Is_Fabric;
				cutW = 0.0;
				cutL = 0.0;
				reqPieces = 0;
				issPieces = 0;
				wasteIssPieces = 0;
				if(isFab == true)
				{
					cutW = ifnull(mr.Cut_Size_Width,0);
					cutL = ifnull(mr.Cut_Size_Length,0);
					reqPieces = ifnull(mr.Required_Pieces,0);
					// Two issued-so-far counters, mirroring Issued_Qty.
					issPieces = ifnull(mr.Pieces_From_Waste,0) + ifnull(mr.Pieces_From_Raw,0);
					wasteIssPieces = ifnull(mr.Pieces_From_Waste,0);
				}

				//--------------------------------------------------------------
				// SOURCE IS PART OF THE KEY, so a reissue can never merge into
				// the plan's own demand for the same material.
				//
				// They are different questions. The plan row says "this order
				// needs 28.35m". A reissue row says "3 panels were ruined at
				// Cutting on 6 Aug and he wants them again". Added together they
				// read as one requirement of 29.55m that was always expected -
				// which hides the damage completely, and is exactly what the
				// store would then be unable to explain.
				//
				// A Creator field that was never written is EMPTY, not null, so
				// every row written before this field existed lands on "Plan".
				// That is correct: they all did come from the plan.
				//--------------------------------------------------------------
				srcTxt = ifnull(mr.Source,"").toString().trim();
				if(srcTxt == "")
				{
					srcTxt = "Plan";
				}
				isReissue = false;
				if(srcTxt == "Reissue")
				{
					isReissue = true;
				}

				// Non-fabric rows carry 0x0, so they collapse to one line per
				// material exactly as before.
				key = supId + "|" + matId + "|" + cutW + "x" + cutL + "|" + srcTxt;

				cur = agg.get(key);
				if(cur == null)
				{
					cur = Map();
					cur.put("supId",supId);
					cur.put("matId",matId);
					cur.put("name",mr.Material_Name);
					cur.put("unit",mr.Unit);
					cur.put("isFabric",isFab);
					cur.put("isReissue",isReissue);
					cur.put("cutW",cutW);
					cur.put("cutL",cutL);
					cur.put("required",0.0);
					cur.put("issued",0.0);
					cur.put("reqPieces",0);
					cur.put("issPieces",0);
					cur.put("wasteIssPieces",0);
					// The aggregate collapses every plan into one number, which is
					// what the screen needs but leaves a shortage untraceable - it
					// cannot say which orders wanted it. One line per requirement
					// row keeps that, and per ROW rather than per plan because a
					// plan can want the same fabric for two different items.
					cur.put("lines",List());
					orderedKeys.add(key);
				}
				matKeyTxt = matId + "";
				if(neededMats.contains(matKeyTxt) == false)
				{
					neededMats.add(matKeyTxt);
				}

				cur.put("required",cur.get("required") + ifnull(mr.Required_Qty,0));
				cur.put("issued",cur.get("issued") + ifnull(mr.Issued_Qty,0));
				cur.put("reqPieces",cur.get("reqPieces") + reqPieces);
				cur.put("issPieces",cur.get("issPieces") + issPieces);
				cur.put("wasteIssPieces",cur.get("wasteIssPieces") + wasteIssPieces);

				// Which ITEM on the order wanted this, and whether it is a QC
				// remake. Without it, an order that has both an original line
				// and a remake line for the same fabric shows two rows reading
				// the same order number and the same material, and the person
				// raising a wash or purchase ticket cannot tell which is which.
				piKey = ifnull(mr.Plan_Item,"") + "";
				piInfo = ifnull(piCache.get(piKey),"").toString();
				if(piInfo == "" && piKey != "")
				{
					piName = "";
					piRemake = "false";
					for each  piRec in Plan_Item[ID == mr.Plan_Item]
					{
						piName = ifnull(piRec.Item_Name,"").toString().trim();
						if(ifnull(piRec.Is_Remake,false) == true)
						{
							piRemake = "true";
						}
					}
					piInfo = piRemake + "|" + piName;
					piCache.put(piKey,piInfo);
				}

				lnItem = "";
				lnRemake = false;
				if(piInfo != "")
				{
					if(piInfo.startsWith("true|"))
					{
						lnRemake = true;
					}
					lnItem = piInfo.substring(piInfo.indexOf("|") + 1);
				}

				lnMap = Map();
				lnMap.put("planId",plan.ID + "");
				lnMap.put("salesOrder",soNo);
				lnMap.put("planItemId",piKey);
				lnMap.put("item",lnItem.replaceAll("\"","'"));
				lnMap.put("isRemake",lnRemake);
				lnMap.put("supervisorId",supId + "");
				lnMap.put("required",ifnull(mr.Required_Qty,0));
				lnMap.put("issued",ifnull(mr.Issued_Qty,0));
				// PIECES PER LINE. Lots are allocated per plan item - one item is
				// one tone - and fulfilment is counted in cut pieces, not metres.
				// The aggregate above cannot answer "how many does THIS item still
				// owe", which is the only question the allocator asks.
				lnMap.put("reqPieces",reqPieces);
				lnMap.put("issPieces",issPieces);
				// THE PIN. Which lot this row's cloth already came off, stamped
				// by issueMaterials on the first issue.
				//
				// The READABLE NUMBER travels with it (issuedLotNo, added at
				// serialisation). An emptied lot is dropped from the lots array
				// above - it is no longer a choice - so when the pinned lot has
				// run out the widget has an id it cannot name, and "already cut
				// from 3819000000141003" is not something to put on a screen.
				//
				// One order is one tone, so once ANY row of an order has taken
				// cloth the rest of that order is committed to the same lot - a
				// remake replacing three ruined panels has to match the
				// ninety-seven already cut, and a second issue against a growing
				// order has to as well. Empty means nothing has been issued yet
				// and the lot is still free to choose.
				lnMap.put("issuedLot",ifnull(mr.Issued_Lot,"").toString().trim());
				// Why the store is being asked for this material a second time.
				// One row per damage line, never summed, so this reason belongs
				// to exactly one incident - which is the whole point of not
				// merging them.
				lnMap.put("reason",ifnull(mr.Reason,"").toString().replaceAll("\"","'").replaceAll("\r","").replaceAll("\n"," | ").replaceAll("\t"," "));
				lnList = cur.get("lines");
				lnList.add(lnMap);
				cur.put("lines",lnList);

				agg.put(key,cur);
			}
			}
			// end of the mr loop for this plan
			}
		}
	}
	// end of the plan walk


	//------------------------------------------------------------------------
	// Available waste, oldest first. That sort IS the FIFO policy - the list is
	// consumed in the order given. Added_Time is Creator's own field, which is
	// why Waste_Master needs no Generated_On of its own.
	//
	// Nothing is claimed here any more. The candidates go out whole and the
	// widget spends them down across the cards in this same priority order, so
	// the first supervisor still sees the real pieces and every later one sees
	// what is genuinely left.
	//------------------------------------------------------------------------
	// Grouped BY SKU, not one flat list.
	//
	// The allocation used to scan every available remnant for every requirement
	// key, and repeat that scan once per pass - so the cost was keys x rows x
	// rows, nearly all of it comparing linen against thread. That is what blew
	// Creator's statement-execution limit, and because that limit is enforced by
	// killing the script it is NOT catchable: the try/catch below never ran and
	// the widget got a bare 500 instead of the error card.
	//
	// Grouping first means a material only ever looks at its own cloth, and a
	// material with no matching remnant does no allocation work at all.
	wasteBySku = Map();

	// WHERE THE OFFCUT IS, keyed by waste id and resolved only at emit time.
	//
	// Kept out of the scoring loop deliberately. That loop runs once per
	// candidate per pass and is the hottest thing in this function; the answer is
	// the same however many times it is scored, so it is looked up once at the
	// end for the handful of pieces actually picked.
	//
	// Lot is a LOOKUP and holds a record id - the readable number comes from the
	// lot pass further down, which is why this stores the id and not a name.
	wasteLotById = Map();
	wasteCartonById = Map();

	// ONE INDEXED QUERY PER MATERIAL IN neededMats, never the whole form.
	//
	// This used to be Waste_Master[Status == "Available"] with NO material
	// filter at all - every available offcut in the whole store, on every
	// single call, regardless of paging. Grouping the RESULT by SKU (below)
	// only controlled how the allocator read it; the FETCH itself scanned
	// unbounded, and paging pendingPlans never touched it because it does not
	// depend on plan count. Confirmed as the real cost by Execute: page 1
	// (20 plans) succeeded, page 2 (20 plans, same size) failed at this exact
	// block - the aggregation loop above is the same shape either way, so a
	// fixed, unbounded cost sitting right after it is what tips a marginal
	// page over the limit.
	//
	// Waste_Master.SKU is a Lookup holding a Raw_Material id, same as
	// Material_Requirement.Material - confirmed against
	// getWastePendingReceipt.dg and getSupervisorWasteReturns.dg, which both
	// do Raw_Material[ID == wp.SKU]. So it can be filtered per material
	// exactly like Raw_Material_Lot[Material == lrMat.toLong()] already is
	// further down in this same function - one indexed query per material
	// actually in play (neededMats, built by the aggregation loop above,
	// bounded by what is on THIS page) instead of one unfiltered scan of
	// every offcut ever declared.
	for each  wNmId in neededMats
	{
		wMatTxt = wNmId.toString().trim();

		availableWaste = Waste_Master[Status == "Available" && SKU == wMatTxt.toLong()] sort by Added_Time;

		for each  wp in availableWaste
		{
			skuId = wMatTxt;

			// Creator fields that were never written are EMPTY, not null, and
			// toDecimal() throws on empty - normalise to string first.
			pwStr = ifnull(wp.Piece_Width,"0").toString().trim();
			if(pwStr == "")
			{
				pwStr = "0";
			}
			plStr = ifnull(wp.Piece_Length,"0").toString().trim();
			if(plStr == "")
			{
				plStr = "0";
			}
			pcStr = ifnull(wp.Piece_Count,"0").toString().trim();
			if(pcStr == "")
			{
				pcStr = "0";
			}

			pw = pwStr.toDecimal();
			pl = plStr.toDecimal();
			pc = pcStr.toDecimal().toLong();

			// A zero-dimension or zero-count row is not stock. It would make the
			// scoring divide by zero, so it never gets into the list.
			if(pw > 0 && pl > 0 && pc > 0)
			{
				wRow = Map();
				wRow.put("wasteId",wp.ID + "");
				wRow.put("sku",skuId);
				wRow.put("width",pw);
				wRow.put("length",pl);
				// The count as it stands on the rack. Nothing here spends it down any
				// more - the widget needs the whole figure to do its own, lot-aware
				// allocation, because a remnant carries the tone of the lot it was
				// cut from and which pieces are usable therefore depends on which lot
				// the fresh cloth comes off.
				wRow.put("count",pc);

				// Which lot it was cut from and which carton it is sitting in. Both
				// are what the store person needs to WALK TO IT - the allocator names
				// a remnant, and without these he is hunting a rack for it.
				wasteLotById.put(wp.ID + "",ifnull(wp.Lot,"").toString().trim());
				wasteCartonById.put(wp.ID + "",ifnull(wp.Carton_Number,"").toString().trim().replaceAll("\"","'"));

				skuList = wasteBySku.get(skuId);
				if(skuList == null)
				{
					skuList = List();
				}
				skuList.add(wRow);
				wasteBySku.put(skuId,skuList);

			}
		}
	}


	//------------------------------------------------------------------------
	// LOTS, fetched ONCE and grouped by material as ready-built JSON fragments.
	//
	// Same discipline as the waste grouping above and for the same reason: a
	// query per material inside the key loop is the shape that blew the
	// statement-execution limit, and that limit is not catchable.
	//
	// NOT decremented across supervisors the way waste is. Waste has to be,
	// because the widget PRE-SELECTS the pieces and two cards offering the same
	// remnant would both promise it. Lots are chosen by hand, so every card
	// showing the true shelf figure is honest - the same convention
	// availableStock already follows. issueMaterials validates against real stock
	// at issue time regardless.
	//
	// Blocked lots are excluded: quarantined cloth must not be offered. A Status
	// never written reads EMPTY, not null, so it is defaulted to Active here
	// rather than filtered in the query, where empty would fall the wrong way.
	//------------------------------------------------------------------------
	lotJsonByMat = Map();

	// Lot id -> the number written on the roll. Filled for every lot of a needed
	// material, INCLUDING blocked and empty ones, which the lot picker below
	// deliberately drops: an offcut cut from a lot that has since been blocked or
	// run dry still has to be able to say which lot it came from.
	lotNameById = Map();

	//------------------------------------------------------------------------
	// PRINTED PIECES, GROUPED BY LOT.
	//
	// A lot whose Form is Pieces holds no continuous cloth: its metres are a
	// MAINTAINED SUM of these rows, kept for valuation and for the lot ranking,
	// and NOTHING PLANS A CUT OFF THEM. Five 3.00 m pieces are not 15 m of usable
	// cloth - against a 55 cm cut each piece yields floor(300/55) = 5 rows and
	// strands 25 cm, so 25 rows where a continuous 15 m roll gives 27. The
	// allocator has to simulate per piece, so it has to be sent the pieces.
	//
	// One query for the whole form, grouped into a map, exactly like the waste
	// pass above and for the same reason: a query per lot is the road to the
	// statement-execution limit, which is not catchable and reaches the widget as
	// a bare 500 with no error card.
	//
	// Bounded the same way Waste_Master is - only Available rows with something
	// on them, which is printed cloth physically on the rack today, not every
	// piece ever received.
	//
	// Added_Time order, so a tie in the allocator's scoring falls to the oldest
	// piece and printed cloth does not age on the rack.
	//------------------------------------------------------------------------
	// UNBOUNDED FETCH REMOVED. This used to be
	// Fabric_Piece[Piece_Status == "Available"] with NO lot filter - every
	// printed-piece row in the whole store, on every call, grouped by Lot
	// only AFTER the fetch. Same bug shape as the Waste_Master fetch above,
	// and it could not be fixed the same way (loop over neededMats and
	// filter per material) because Fabric_Piece is keyed by LOT, and lot ids
	// are not known until the per-material lot loop below runs.
	//
	// So it moves there instead, and lazily: pieceMetersByLot / pieceJsonByLot
	// (below) are only ever read when a lot's Form is "Pieces" - most lots are
	// Roll and never need this at all. Querying Fabric_Piece[Lot == lotR.ID]
	// right at that read, one lot at a time, costs a query only for the lots
	// that are actually Pieces-form AND actually in play on this page -
	// bounded twice over instead of not at all.

	//------------------------------------------------------------------------
	// THE MATERIAL RECORD ITSELF, ONCE PER MATERIAL IN PLAY.
	//
	// This read used to sit inside the emit loop below, which walks KEYS - one
	// per supervisor x material x cut size x source - so the same fabric on
	// three cards was fetched three times. Hoisted here it is one indexed query
	// per material, and the emit loop reads the maps.
	//
	// It also has to happen BEFORE the lot pass, because of Print_Base.
	//
	// Raw_Material.Print_Base is set on the PRINTED material and names the plain
	// SKU it is printed from. It is what makes the plain->printed link navigable
	// in both directions, and without it no screen can say "there is no printed
	// stock, but there is plain cloth to print" - a row that goes blank while
	// cloth sits on the rack is exactly the silent state this screen exists to
	// kill.
	//
	// The base's LOTS are built by the ordinary lot pass below rather than by a
	// second copy of it: a base already in play is never fetched twice, and one
	// that is not simply joins the list that pass walks. A query per material
	// inside a row loop is the shape that blows the statement-execution limit,
	// which is not catchable and reaches the widget as a bare 500.
	//------------------------------------------------------------------------
	matSkuByMat = Map();
	matDispByMat = Map();
	matQtyByMat = Map();
	matWidthByMat = Map();
	printBaseByMat = Map();
	// Display name with a fallback, for the PLAIN base only. The name a
	// requirement row captured at plan time does not exist for a material
	// nothing on this screen is asking for, so an empty display name has to fall
	// back to something rather than print a blank on the row.
	baseNameById = Map();
	lotMats = List();
	baseMats = List();

	for each  nmId in neededMats
	{
		nmTxt = nmId.toString().trim();
		if(nmTxt != "" && lotMats.contains(nmTxt) == false)
		{
			lotMats.add(nmTxt);
		}
		for each  rmB in Raw_Material[ID == nmTxt.toLong()]
		{
			matSkuByMat.put(nmTxt,ifnull(rmB.SKU,"").toString());
			// Trimmed only - the serialisation below does the quote escaping for
			// this one, exactly as it did when it was read inline.
			bDisp = ifnull(rmB.Material_Display_Name,"").toString().trim();
			matDispByMat.put(nmTxt,bDisp);
			matQtyByMat.put(nmTxt,ifnull(rmB.Quantity,0));

			// Stored as TEXT, in inches. Converted here and nowhere else.
			bwIn = ifnull(rmB.Fabric_Width_Inches,"").toString().trim();
			if(bwIn == "")
			{
				bwIn = "0";
			}
			matWidthByMat.put(nmTxt,bwIn.toDecimal() * 2.54);

			bName = bDisp;
			if(bName == "")
			{
				bName = ifnull(rmB.Name,"").toString().trim();
			}
			if(bName == "")
			{
				bName = ifnull(rmB.SKU,"").toString().trim();
			}
			// Free text crossing hand-built JSON needs the WHOLE chain. A
			// Material_Display_Name is typed by hand in Creator and one press of
			// Enter in it puts a raw newline inside a JSON string literal, which
			// is invalid JSON and fails with no error card at all.
			baseNameById.put(nmTxt,bName.replaceAll("\"","'").replaceAll("\r","").replaceAll("\n"," | ").replaceAll("\t"," "));

			pbTxt = ifnull(rmB.Print_Base,"").toString().trim();
			printBaseByMat.put(nmTxt,pbTxt);
			if(pbTxt != "" && baseMats.contains(pbTxt) == false)
			{
				baseMats.add(pbTxt);
			}
		}
	}

	// The bases nothing on this screen is asking for. Added to the lot list AFTER
	// it is complete, so a base that is itself in play is not queued twice - two
	// passes of the lot builder over one material would emit its lots twice and
	// the allocator would offer the same cloth as two lots.
	for each  bmId in baseMats
	{
		if(lotMats.contains(bmId) == false)
		{
			lotMats.add(bmId);
			for each  rmC in Raw_Material[ID == bmId.toLong()]
			{
				cName = ifnull(rmC.Material_Display_Name,"").toString().trim();
				if(cName == "")
				{
					cName = ifnull(rmC.Name,"").toString().trim();
				}
				if(cName == "")
				{
					cName = ifnull(rmC.SKU,"").toString().trim();
				}
				baseNameById.put(bmId,cName.replaceAll("\"","'").replaceAll("\r","").replaceAll("\n"," | ").replaceAll("\t"," "));
			}
		}
	}

	//------------------------------------------------------------------------
	// ONE INDEXED QUERY PER MATERIAL IN PLAY, never the whole form.
	//
	// This used to fetch every Raw_Material_Lot ever created and test each row
	// against neededMats in code. The test was cheap per row; the FETCH was
	// not. Lots accumulate with every purchase for ever and an emptied lot is
	// never deleted - it stays as a zero row so history still resolves - so the
	// cost grew with purchase history on the screen the store person reloads
	// all day. At ~13 lots a material and a thousand materials that is thirteen
	// thousand rows fetched to use a few dozen, and the statement-execution
	// limit is NOT catchable: it kills the script and the widget gets a bare
	// 500 with no error card.
	//
	// neededMats is built above from the requirements actually on this screen,
	// so it is bounded by OPEN WORK rather than by history - the same property
	// that makes the rest of this function scale. Creator filters on the
	// indexed lookup before it fetches, so cost now tracks what is on screen.
	//
	// ORDERING IS UNCHANGED WHERE IT CAN BE OBSERVED. Everything below is
	// grouped by material into lotJsonByMat, so within-material Added_Time
	// order is the only order the widget ever sees, and the inner query still
	// carries it. The old global sort across materials was never visible.
	//
	// The lotNameById write below still happens BEFORE the Blocked and
	// empty-lot filters, exactly as it did, so an offcut can still name its lot
	// after that lot has dropped off the picker.
	//------------------------------------------------------------------------
	calcWashByMat = Map();
	calcUnwashByMat = Map();
	calcInWashByMat = Map();

	// lotMats, not neededMats: it is neededMats plus any PLAIN BASE of a
	// printed material on this screen, so the base's lots are built by this one
	// pass and not by a second copy of it.
	for each  lotMatId in lotMats
	{
		lrMat = lotMatId.toString().trim();

		// sort by on its own assignment, never inline in a for each header.
		matLotRows = Raw_Material_Lot[Material == lrMat.toLong()] sort by Added_Time;
		
		matWash = 0.0;
		matUnwash = 0.0;
		matInWash = 0.0;

		for each  lotR in matLotRows
		{

		// Before the Blocked and empty-lot filters, so an offcut can still name
		// its lot after that lot itself has dropped off the picker.
		lotNameById.put(lotR.ID + "",ifnull(lotR.Lot_Number,"").toString().trim().replaceAll("\"","'"));

		lrSt = ifnull(lotR.Status,"").toString().trim();
		if(lrSt == "")
		{
			lrSt = "Active";
		}

		// BLOCKED LOTS ARE SENT, FLAGGED - not dropped.
		//
		// Quarantined cloth must never be allocated, and the widget does not
		// allocate it. But dropping the row entirely left a screen saying "nothing
		// on the rack" while the store person stood in front of eighteen metres of
		// the stuff, which reads as the screen being broken and is the fastest way
		// to lose his trust in every other figure on it.
		//
		// The flag travels; the allocator refuses it and the row names it.
		lrBlocked = "false";
		if(lrSt == "Blocked")
		{
			lrBlocked = "true";
		}

		// Creator numerics never written are EMPTY, not null, and toDecimal()
		// throws on empty - normalise to string first.
		lrwS = ifnull(lotR.Wash_Quantity,"0").toString().trim();
		if(lrwS == "")
		{
			lrwS = "0";
		}
		lruS = ifnull(lotR.Unwash_Quantity,"0").toString().trim();
		if(lruS == "")
		{
			lruS = "0";
		}
		// At the wash house: not issuable, not on the rack either. Shown so
		// a lot that looks empty explains itself.
		lriS = ifnull(lotR.In_Wash_Qty,"0").toString().trim();
		if(lriS == "")
		{
			lriS = "0";
		}

		lrWash = lrwS.toDecimal();
		lrUnwash = lruS.toDecimal();
		
		// FORM DECIDES HOW THE ALLOCATOR READS THE METRES, and an EMPTY one
		// means Roll. Every lot that existed before printing has it blank,
		// and reading blank as anything else would put the whole rack down
		// the piece path with no pieces - every roll suddenly uncuttable.
		lrForm = ifnull(lotR.Form,"").toString().trim();
		if(lrForm != "Pieces")
		{
			lrForm = "Roll";
		}
		
		// A Pieces lot holds no continuous cloth, its usable stock is exactly
		// the sum of the pieces on the rack. The Creator rollup misses scripted
		// additions, so we sum them live from the pieces we just fetched.
		//
		// QUERIED HERE, ONE LOT AT A TIME, ONLY FOR Pieces-FORM LOTS - see the
		// note above the lot loop for why this moved from an unbounded
		// upfront Fabric_Piece fetch. lrPieces is built in the SAME pass as
		// lrWash so a Pieces lot costs one Fabric_Piece query, not two.
		lrPieces = "";
		if(lrForm == "Pieces")
		{
			lrPieceMtr = 0.0;
			for each  fp in Fabric_Piece[Lot == lotR.ID && Piece_Status == "Available"] sort by Added_Time
			{
				// Creator fields never written are EMPTY, not null, and
				// toDecimal() throws on empty - normalise to string, test,
				// then convert.
				fpcS = ifnull(fp.Piece_Count,"0").toString().trim();
				if(fpcS == "")
				{
					fpcS = "0";
				}
				fplS = ifnull(fp.Piece_Length_Cm,"0").toString().trim();
				if(fplS == "")
				{
					fplS = "0";
				}
				fpwS = ifnull(fp.Piece_Width_Cm,"0").toString().trim();
				if(fpwS == "")
				{
					fpwS = "0";
				}

				// GREIGE PIECES ARE SENT, NOT DROPPED, and the allocator
				// refuses them. Same rule as an unwashed roll: greige never
				// counts as available today. Dropping them would make a lot
				// holding nothing but unwashed cloth read as empty, which is
				// the "nothing on the rack" lie the blocked-lot note above
				// exists to prevent.
				fpSt = ifnull(fp.State,"").toString().trim();
				if(fpSt == "")
				{
					fpSt = "Wash";
				}

				if(fpcS.toDecimal() > 0 && fplS.toDecimal() > 0)
				{
					fpFrag = "{\"pieceId\":\"" + fp.ID + "\",\"lengthCm\":" + fplS.toDecimal() + ",\"widthCm\":" + fpwS.toDecimal() + ",\"count\":" + fpcS.toDecimal() + ",\"state\":\"" + fpSt + "\",\"carton\":\"" + ifnull(fp.Carton_Number,"").toString().trim().replaceAll("\"","'") + "\"}";
					if(lrPieces != "")
					{
						lrPieces = lrPieces + ",";
					}
					lrPieces = lrPieces + fpFrag;

					if(fpSt == "Wash")
					{
						lrPieceMtr = lrPieceMtr + ((fplS.toDecimal() / 100) * fpcS.toDecimal());
					}
				}
			}
			lrWash = lrPieceMtr;
		}

		// An empty lot is not a choice. It stays on the form for its history
		// but must not clutter a screen about what can be issued today -
		// unless its cloth is at the wash, which is exactly why it looks
		// empty and exactly what the store person needs told.
		if(lrWash > 0 || lrUnwash > 0 || lriS.toDecimal() > 0)
		{
			// Fabric is issued as WASHED metres - issueMaterials reads
			// Wash_Quantity for fabric - so wash is the issuable figure and
			// unwash is context, exactly as unwashedStock is at material level.
			lrNum = ifnull(lotR.Lot_Number,"").toString().replaceAll("\"","'");
			lrLab = ifnull(lotR.Lot_Label,"").toString().replaceAll("\"","'");

			// Ids as strings - an 18-digit id breaks JSON.parse on the widget.
			lotFrag = "{\"lotId\":\"" + lotR.ID + "\",\"lotNumber\":\"" + lrNum + "\",\"label\":\"" + lrLab + "\",\"blocked\":" + lrBlocked + ",\"wash\":" + lrWash + ",\"unwash\":" + lrUnwash + ",\"inWash\":" + lriS.toDecimal() + ",\"form\":\"" + lrForm + "\",\"pieces\":[" + lrPieces + "]}";

			prevLots = ifnull(lotJsonByMat.get(lrMat),"").toString();
			if(prevLots != "")
			{
				prevLots = prevLots + ",";
			}
			lotJsonByMat.put(lrMat,prevLots + lotFrag);
		}
		
		// Accumulate total stock across all lots (including blocked and empty, mimicking the Creator rollup
		// but guaranteeing it is perfectly in sync with the lots the UI receives, notably printed pieces).
		matWash = matWash + lrWash;
		matUnwash = matUnwash + lrUnwash;
		matInWash = matInWash + lriS.toDecimal();

		}
		// end of the lots for this material
		
		calcWashByMat.put(lrMat, matWash);
		calcUnwashByMat.put(lrMat, matUnwash);
		calcInWashByMat.put(lrMat, matInWash);
	}
	// end of the per-material loop


	//------------------------------------------------------------------------
	// Open exception tickets, fetched ONCE and grouped by material.
	//
	// This was a query plus a subform walk inside the per-material loop, so a
	// screen with thirty materials issued thirty queries to read a table that
	// rarely holds more than a handful of rows.
	//------------------------------------------------------------------------
	excBySku = Map();
	for each  oex in Material_Exception[Status == "Open"]
	{
		exT = ifnull(oex.Type_field,"").toString();
		exSku = ifnull(oex.SKU,"").toString();

		if(exT != "" && exSku != "")
		{
			coveredPlans = List();
			for each  exLn in oex.Exception_Lines
			{
				pId = ifnull(exLn.Plan,"").toString();
				if(pId != "" && coveredPlans.contains(pId) == false)
				{
					coveredPlans.add(pId);
				}
			}

			exMap = Map();
			exMap.put("type",exT);
			exMap.put("planIds",coveredPlans);
			// WHICH LOT'S GREIGE THIS TICKET IS FOR. A wash ticket belongs to a
			// lot, not to a material: one fabric can have jobs waiting on two
			// shades at once and each needs its own ticket. Matched on the
			// material alone, raising the first greyed out the second lot's
			// button and that shade was never queued at all.
			//
			// A lookup holds a record id, so this is the Raw_Material_Lot id as
			// text. Empty on a purchase ticket - there is no cloth to name yet -
			// and on any wash ticket raised before the field existed.
			exMap.put("lot",ifnull(oex.Lot,"").toString().trim());

			exList = excBySku.get(exSku);
			if(exList == null)
			{
				exList = List();
			}
			exList.add(exMap);
			excBySku.put(exSku,exList);
		}
	}


	bySup = Map();
	supOrder = List();

	for each  k in orderedKeys
	{

		e = agg.get(k);
		supId = e.get("supId");
		matId = e.get("matId");
		// Computed ONCE. This was being recomputed for every candidate remnant
		// on every allocation pass.
		matIdTxt = matId.toString();
		isFab = e.get("isFabric");
		availableStock = 0.0;
		unwashedStock = 0.0;
		inWashStock = 0.0;
		fabricWidthCm = 0.0;
		skuText = "";
		// Read live from Raw_Material rather than the Material_Name snapshot on
		// the requirement, so renaming a material fixes plans already created.
		dispName = "";
		// Read off the per-material maps built above rather than queried again
		// here. This loop walks KEYS, so a fabric wanted by three supervisors used
		// to fetch the same Raw_Material record three times.
		skuText = ifnull(matSkuByMat.get(matIdTxt),"");
		dispName = ifnull(matDispByMat.get(matIdTxt),"").toString().trim();
		if(isFab == true)
		{
			// Bypass the Creator rollup field which lags and ignores printed piece lot additions.
			// Use the synchronous sums calculated directly from the lots we just sent.
			availableStock = ifnull(calcWashByMat.get(matIdTxt), 0.0);
			unwashedStock = ifnull(calcUnwashByMat.get(matIdTxt), 0.0);
			inWashStock = ifnull(calcInWashByMat.get(matIdTxt), 0.0);
			fabricWidthCm = ifnull(matWidthByMat.get(matIdTxt), 0.0);
		}
		else
		{
			availableStock = ifnull(matQtyByMat.get(matIdTxt),0);
		}


		req = e.get("required");
		iss = e.get("issued");

		// Display name where one is set, otherwise the name captured at plan
		// time - an empty display name must not blank out the row.
		showName = e.get("name");
		if(dispName != "")
		{
			showName = dispName;
		}

		matEntry = Map();
		matEntry.put("materialId",matId + "");
		matEntry.put("material",showName);
		matEntry.put("sku",skuText);
		matEntry.put("unit",e.get("unit"));
		matEntry.put("isFabric",isFab);
		// Set on the aggregate rather than derived per line: Source is part of
		// the key, so every line under one entry has the same one by
		// construction. Reading it off a line would be true today and quietly
		// wrong the moment that key changes.
		matEntry.put("isReissue",ifnull(e.get("isReissue"),false));
		matEntry.put("required",req);
		matEntry.put("issued",iss);
		matEntry.put("remaining",req - iss);
		matEntry.put("availableStock",availableStock);
		// Carried through untouched so the widget can attach it to an exception.
		matEntry.put("lines",e.get("lines"));

		// Which requests are already open for this material, and WHICH PLANS each
		// one covers. The plan list is the point: a ticket raised yesterday for
		// two orders says nothing about an order that appeared this morning, so
		// the widget re-arms the button when it sees demand the ticket predates.
		// Without this, new demand would silently inherit an old request.
		//
		// Read off the map built above rather than queried again per material.
		openExList = excBySku.get(matIdTxt);
		if(openExList == null)
		{
			openExList = List();
		}
		matEntry.put("openExceptions",openExList);


		if(isFab == true)
		{
			cutW = e.get("cutW");
			cutL = e.get("cutL");
			outstandingPieces = e.get("reqPieces") - e.get("issPieces");
			if(outstandingPieces < 0)
			{
				outstandingPieces = 0;
			}

			matEntry.put("unwashedStock",unwashedStock);
			matEntry.put("inWashStock",inWashStock);
			matEntry.put("cutWidth",cutW);
			matEntry.put("cutLength",cutL);
			matEntry.put("fabricWidthCm",fabricWidthCm);
			matEntry.put("requiredPieces",e.get("reqPieces"));
			matEntry.put("issuedPieces",e.get("issPieces"));
			matEntry.put("wasteIssuedPieces",e.get("wasteIssPieces"));
			matEntry.put("outstandingPieces",outstandingPieces);

			// Built once per material above and reused here, so the same fabric
			// appearing on three supervisor cards costs one build, not three.
			matEntry.put("lotsJson",ifnull(lotJsonByMat.get(matIdTxt),"").toString());

			//----------------------------------------------------------
			// THE PLAIN CLOTH THIS PRINTED SKU IS PRINTED FROM.
			//
			// Empty on plain cloth, which is every fabric row that is not a
			// printed SKU - the widget reads the empty string as "this is not
			// printed" and the reason never fires.
			//
			// The base lots go out in the SAME SHAPE as this row's own lots,
			// because they were built by the same pass. A row with no printed
			// stock can then name the lot the plain cloth is sitting on and how
			// much of it there is, which is the whole point: a row that goes
			// blank while cloth is on the rack reads as the screen being broken.
			//----------------------------------------------------------
			pbForMat = ifnull(printBaseByMat.get(matIdTxt),"").toString().trim();
			pbName = "";
			pbLotsJson = "";
			if(pbForMat != "")
			{
				pbName = ifnull(baseNameById.get(pbForMat),"").toString();
				pbLotsJson = ifnull(lotJsonByMat.get(pbForMat),"").toString();
			}
			matEntry.put("printBase",pbForMat);
			matEntry.put("printBaseName",pbName);
			matEntry.put("printBaseLotsJson",pbLotsJson);

			wastePicks = List();

			//----------------------------------------------------------
			// PIECES STILL TO COVER. Set here, BEFORE the waste
			// allocation, and decremented by it.
			//
			// This used to live inside a "has this material any offcuts"
			// guard, together with the fresh-metres sum that reads it - so a
			// material with no remnants on the rack skipped both, and shipped
			// a stale "Required_Qty - Issued_Qty" instead. That went wrong in
			// two directions at once:
			//
			//  UNDER-ASKS after any part-issue. Cloth is only usable in
			//  whole marker rows, so issuing 10m of a 55cm cut yields 18
			//  rows and throws 10cm away - but the budget was charged the
			//  full 10m. The next ask came back 10cm short of the row it
			//  needed, and the last piece could never be issued at all.
			//
			//  OVER-ASKS badly once remnants have been used and are gone.
			//  36 of 100 pieces cut from offcuts leaves 64 needing cloth -
			//  12.10m - but the stale figure still quoted the plan's full
			//  18.70m. The widget pre-fills it and issueMaterials accepts
			//  it, so 6.60m walked out with nothing booking it.
			//
			// Both are the same mistake: budgeting fabric in METRES.
			// CLAUDE.md already states the rule - Required_Qty is a
			// pre-waste estimate, Required_Pieces is the target - and this
			// is what makes the store screen obey it.
			//----------------------------------------------------------
			remainPcs = outstandingPieces;

			// Cut pieces the waste covers. NOT the same as the number of waste
			// pieces handed over - one 300x400 remnant can yield two 187x137
			// cuts, so 1 piece issued can cover 2 pieces required.
			coveredPcsTotal = 0;


			// THE WASTE ALLOCATION USED TO LIVE HERE, and it has moved to the
			// widget. It was a least-waste-area scan, once per candidate remnant per
			// pass per requirement key - and it was lot-BLIND, because at this point
			// no lot has been chosen yet.
			//
			// That blindness is what made it wrong rather than merely expensive. A
			// remnant carries the tone of the lot it was cut from, so which remnants
			// are usable depends on which lot the fresh cloth comes off - and that is
			// decided in the widget. Choosing the lot and choosing the offcuts is ONE
			// decision, so it happens in one place.
			//
			// The candidates now travel raw in wasteStock, with their lot and carton,
			// and the widget allocates them alongside the fresh metres.
			//
			// Removing it takes keys x rows x rows statements out of the function
			// closest to Creator's statement-execution limit - the limit that is NOT
			// catchable, kills the script, and hands the widget a bare 500 with no
			// error card. That is a real win, not a side effect.
			//
			// remainPcs is therefore left at the full outstanding count and the fresh
			// metres below are the pre-waste figure. The widget overwrites both in
			// applyLotAllocation before anything renders; leaving them honest rather
			// than zero means a stale widget over-asks instead of under-asking, which
			// is the safe direction to be wrong in.

			//----------------------------------------------------------
			// Whatever the waste could not cover comes off fresh fabric.
			//
			// OUTSIDE the guard above, so this runs for EVERY fabric row -
			// remnants or none. With no remnants the allocation simply did
			// not touch remainPcs, and this asks for exactly the cloth the
			// outstanding pieces need.
			//----------------------------------------------------------
			perRowR = 0;
			canCount = false;
			if(e.get("reqPieces") > 0 && cutW > 0 && cutL > 0 && fabricWidthCm > 0)
			{
				perRowR = (fabricWidthCm / cutW).floor();
				if(perRowR > 0)
				{
					canCount = true;
				}
			}

			if(canCount == true)
			{
				freshMeters = 0.0;
				if(remainPcs > 0)
				{
					// * 1.0 for the same reason as takePcs above - an integer
					// quotient is truncated before .ceil() can round it up,
					// which would ask for one row too few of fresh cloth.
					rowsR = ((remainPcs * 1.0) / perRowR).ceil();
					freshMeters = (rowsR * cutL) / 100;
				}
			}
			else
			{
				// No piece data to count with - a row planned before
				// Required_Pieces existed, a cut wider than the cloth, or a
				// fabric whose width was never recorded. The metres estimate
				// is all there is, and quoting 0 would make the row
				// unissuable rather than merely approximate.
				freshMeters = req - iss;
				if(freshMeters < 0)
				{
					freshMeters = 0.0;
				}
			}

			matEntry.put("wastePicks",wastePicks);
			matEntry.put("freshMeters",freshMeters);
			matEntry.put("piecesCoveredByWaste",coveredPcsTotal);
			// Pieces that still have to be cut from fresh fabric. This, not
			// outstandingPieces, is what the store person is being asked for.
			matEntry.put("freshPieces",outstandingPieces - coveredPcsTotal);

			// Required, as shown to the store person, is the FRESH requirement.
			// The plan-time Required_Qty assumed no waste existed, so quoting it
			// here would ask for more fabric than the cutting now needs.
			matEntry.put("required",freshMeters);
			matEntry.put("requiredTotal",req);

			// For fabric, "how much is left to issue" is the waste-adjusted
			// figure, not Required_Qty - Issued_Qty. Without this override a
			// requirement fully covered by waste would need 0 metres, be issued
			// 0 metres, and then sit on the screen as pending forever because
			// the metres never move. Fulfilment for fabric is counted in
			// pieces; Required_Qty is an estimate, not a target.
			matEntry.put("remaining",freshMeters);
		}

		matList = bySup.get(supId);
		if(matList == null)
		{
			matList = List();
			supOrder.add(supId);
		}
		matList.add(matEntry);
		bySup.put(supId,matList);
	}


	//------------------------------------------------------------------------
	// Serialisation, BY HAND.
	//
	// This used to be Map.toString() on a Map holding a List of Maps. That
	// worked for as long as the deepest level happened to be empty, and broke
	// the day the first waste piece existed: wastePicks added a fourth level of
	// nesting, Deluge emitted Java map syntax rather than JSON for it, and the
	// oversized malformed string failed on the way out of the function - AFTER
	// the try block, which is why the widget saw a bare 500 instead of the
	// error card this function is careful to return.
	//
	// Every other function in this app builds its JSON by hand for exactly this
	// reason. This one was the last holdout.
	//------------------------------------------------------------------------
	resultJson = "[";
	isFirst = true;
	// supOrder, not bySup.keys() - the cards must render in the same priority
	// order the waste was allocated in, or the allocation looks arbitrary.
	for each  supId in supOrder
	{
		supName = "Unknown";
		for each  emp in Employee[ID == supId]
		{
			supName = emp.Employee_Name;
		}

		matsJson = "";
		for each  mEnt in bySup.get(supId)
		{
			if(matsJson != "")
			{
				matsJson = matsJson + ",";
			}

			linesJson = "";
			for each  lnE in mEnt.get("lines")
			{
				if(linesJson != "")
				{
					linesJson = linesJson + ",";
				}
				linesJson = linesJson + "{\"planId\":\"" + lnE.get("planId") + "\",\"salesOrder\":\"" + lnE.get("salesOrder").toString().replaceAll("\"","'") + "\",\"planItemId\":\"" + lnE.get("planItemId") + "\",\"item\":\"" + lnE.get("item") + "\",\"isRemake\":" + lnE.get("isRemake") + ",\"supervisorId\":\"" + lnE.get("supervisorId") + "\",\"required\":" + lnE.get("required") + ",\"issued\":" + lnE.get("issued") + ",\"reqPieces\":" + lnE.get("reqPieces") + ",\"issPieces\":" + lnE.get("issPieces") + ",\"issuedLot\":\"" + ifnull(lnE.get("issuedLot"),"").toString() + "\",\"issuedLotNo\":\"" + ifnull(lotNameById.get(ifnull(lnE.get("issuedLot"),"").toString().trim()),"").toString() + "\",\"reason\":\"" + lnE.get("reason") + "\"}";
			}

			exsJson = "";
			for each  exE in mEnt.get("openExceptions")
			{
				if(exsJson != "")
				{
					exsJson = exsJson + ",";
				}
				pidsJson = "";
				for each  pidE in exE.get("planIds")
				{
					if(pidsJson != "")
					{
						pidsJson = pidsJson + ",";
					}
					pidsJson = pidsJson + "\"" + pidE + "\"";
				}
				exsJson = exsJson + "{\"type\":\"" + exE.get("type").toString().replaceAll("\"","'") + "\",\"lot\":\"" + ifnull(exE.get("lot"),"").toString() + "\",\"planIds\":[" + pidsJson + "]}";
			}

			matsJson = matsJson + "{\"materialId\":\"" + mEnt.get("materialId") + "\",\"material\":\"" + mEnt.get("material").toString().replaceAll("\"","'") + "\",\"sku\":\"" + mEnt.get("sku").toString().replaceAll("\"","'") + "\",\"unit\":\"" + mEnt.get("unit").toString().replaceAll("\"","'") + "\",\"isFabric\":" + mEnt.get("isFabric") + ",\"isReissue\":" + mEnt.get("isReissue") + ",\"required\":" + mEnt.get("required") + ",\"issued\":" + mEnt.get("issued") + ",\"remaining\":" + mEnt.get("remaining") + ",\"availableStock\":" + mEnt.get("availableStock") + ",\"lines\":[" + linesJson + "],\"openExceptions\":[" + exsJson + "]";

			// The fabric-only half. Emitted only when it was populated, so a
			// non-fabric row carries exactly the keys it did before.
			if(mEnt.get("isFabric") == true)
			{
				picksJson = "";
				for each  pkE in mEnt.get("wastePicks")
				{
					if(picksJson != "")
					{
						picksJson = picksJson + ",";
					}
					// WHERE TO WALK TO. Resolved here, once per piece actually
					// picked, rather than carried through the scoring loop.
					pkWid = pkE.get("wasteId").toString();
					pkLotId = ifnull(wasteLotById.get(pkWid),"").toString().trim();
					pkLotNo = "";
					if(pkLotId != "")
					{
						pkLotNo = ifnull(lotNameById.get(pkLotId),"").toString().trim();
					}
					pkCarton = ifnull(wasteCartonById.get(pkWid),"").toString().trim();

					// The level that broke it. Ids as strings, everything else
					// numeric - the widget round-trips wasteId untouched.
					picksJson = picksJson + "{\"wasteId\":\"" + pkWid + "\",\"width\":" + pkE.get("width") + ",\"length\":" + pkE.get("length") + ",\"pieces\":" + pkE.get("pieces") + ",\"lot\":\"" + pkLotNo + "\",\"carton\":\"" + pkCarton + "\"}";
				}

				//----------------------------------------------------------
				// EVERY REMNANT OF THIS MATERIAL, UNALLOCATED, WITH ITS LOT.
				//
				// The picks above are this function's own allocation, made
				// before any lot is chosen and therefore lot-blind. A remnant
				// carries the tone of the lot it was cut from, so which pieces
				// are usable depends on which lot the fresh cloth comes off -
				// and that is decided in the widget, because the lot allocator
				// lives there and the two are one decision, not two.
				//
				// So the candidates travel raw and the widget allocates them
				// alongside the fresh metres. Counts are the full rack figure,
				// not what this function's own pass left behind.
				//----------------------------------------------------------
				stockJson = "";
				myRack = wasteBySku.get(mEnt.get("materialId").toString());
				if(myRack != null)
				{
					for each  wsE in myRack
					{
						if(stockJson != "")
						{
							stockJson = stockJson + ",";
						}
						wsWid = wsE.get("wasteId").toString();
						wsLotId = ifnull(wasteLotById.get(wsWid),"").toString().trim();
						wsLotNo = "";
						if(wsLotId != "")
						{
							wsLotNo = ifnull(lotNameById.get(wsLotId),"").toString().trim();
						}
						stockJson = stockJson + "{\"wasteId\":\"" + wsWid + "\",\"width\":" + wsE.get("width") + ",\"length\":" + wsE.get("length") + ",\"pieces\":" + wsE.get("count") + ",\"lotId\":\"" + wsLotId + "\",\"lot\":\"" + wsLotNo + "\",\"carton\":\"" + ifnull(wasteCartonById.get(wsWid),"").toString() + "\"}";
					}
				}

				matsJson = matsJson + ",\"unwashedStock\":" + mEnt.get("unwashedStock") + ",\"inWashStock\":" + ifnull(mEnt.get("inWashStock"),0) + ",\"cutWidth\":" + mEnt.get("cutWidth") + ",\"cutLength\":" + mEnt.get("cutLength") + ",\"fabricWidthCm\":" + mEnt.get("fabricWidthCm") + ",\"requiredPieces\":" + mEnt.get("requiredPieces") + ",\"issuedPieces\":" + mEnt.get("issuedPieces") + ",\"wasteIssuedPieces\":" + mEnt.get("wasteIssuedPieces") + ",\"outstandingPieces\":" + mEnt.get("outstandingPieces") + ",\"wastePicks\":[" + picksJson + "],\"wasteStock\":[" + stockJson + "],\"freshMeters\":" + mEnt.get("freshMeters") + ",\"piecesCoveredByWaste\":" + mEnt.get("piecesCoveredByWaste") + ",\"freshPieces\":" + mEnt.get("freshPieces") + ",\"requiredTotal\":" + mEnt.get("requiredTotal") + ",\"lots\":[" + ifnull(mEnt.get("lotsJson"),"").toString() + "],\"printBase\":\"" + ifnull(mEnt.get("printBase"),"").toString() + "\",\"printBaseName\":\"" + ifnull(mEnt.get("printBaseName"),"").toString() + "\",\"printBaseLots\":[" + ifnull(mEnt.get("printBaseLotsJson"),"").toString() + "]";
			}

			matsJson = matsJson + "}";
		}

		if(isFirst == false)
		{
			resultJson = resultJson + ",";
		}
		resultJson = resultJson + "{\"supervisorId\":\"" + supId + "\",\"supervisorName\":\"" + supName.toString().replaceAll("\"","'") + "\",\"materials\":[" + matsJson + "]}";

		isFirst = false;
	}
	resultJson = resultJson + "]";

	// WRAPPED, NOT A BARE ARRAY ANY MORE. plansConsumed is the whole reason
	// for the wrapper: pages are now variable-length (bounded by rowBudget,
	// not by a fixed plan count), so the caller cannot compute where this
	// call stopped on its own - it has to be told. The next call's
	// skipCountTxt is this call's skipCount + plansConsumed.
	resultJson = "{\"plans\":" + resultJson + ",\"plansConsumed\":" + plansConsumed + "}";

	}
	catch (e)
	{
		info "DELUGE ERROR: " + e.toString();
		// Creator reports every failure to a widget as the useless "code 9430",
		// so the real message is carried out in the supervisor name - the only
		// text channel this API's contract has. plansConsumed:0 on error, so
		// the widget's page loop stops rather than retrying the same failing
		// call for ever.
		return "{\"plans\":[{\"supervisorId\":\"0\",\"supervisorName\":\"ERR: " + e.toString().replaceAll("\"","'") + "\",\"materials\":[]}],\"plansConsumed\":0}";
	}

	return resultJson;
}
